egend, but it reflects a real [38;2;255;255;255m          [38;2;238;238;238mantiquity. For centuries tea was regarded less as a drink than as a medicine, chewed, brewed, and prescribed by [38;2;255;255;255m        [38;2;238;238;238mherbalists for everything from fatigue to poor digestion.[18X[18C[38;2;255;255;255m                                                                                 [19X[19C [19X[19C                                             [38;2;238;238;238mThe drink took its cultural shape under the Tang dynasty, which ruled China from 618 to 907. Tea spread from [38;2;255;255;255m           [38;2;238;238;238mmonasteries and the aristocracy to ordinary households, and in the 000s the scholar Lu Yu wrote the Cha Jing, or [38;2;255;255;255m       [38;2;238;238;238mClassic of Tea, a treatise covering cultivation, preparation, and the proper spirit in which to drink it. Lu Yu's[38;2;255;255;255m       [38;2;238;238;238mwork did for tea something like what later connoisseurs would do for[25X[25C[38;2;255;255;255m                      
